(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["anatomy-exam-2-vascular-lymphatics-quiz-version-2"]={"title":"Arteries, Veins, and Lymphatic Systems — Anatomy Exam 2 — Set 2","category":"Anatomy","questions":[{"q":"What are the two main components of blood, and what does the \"formed elements\" component include?","choices":["Plasma and serum only, with formed elements being entirely absent from whole blood","Formed elements only, with no liquid plasma component present in whole blood at all","Plasma and formed elements; formed elements include erythrocytes, leukocytes, and platelets","Only erythrocytes present, with no other formed elements found in whole blood"],"answer":2,"exp":"Blood consists of two components: plasma (the liquid matrix) and formed elements (blood cells and cell fragments suspended in the plasma), including erythrocytes (red blood cells), leukocytes (white blood cells), and platelets. — Anatomy - Vascular & Lymphatics - Shah Summer I UPDATEDsv-1.pptx, Slide 74"},{"q":"What are the functions of erythrocytes, leukocytes, and platelets, respectively?","choices":["All three formed elements share an identical function, each transporting oxygen and carbon dioxide equally","Platelets transport oxygen and carbon dioxide; erythrocytes are instead involved in blood clotting","Erythrocytes transport oxygen/carbon dioxide; leukocytes function in the immune system; platelets are involved in blood clotting","Erythrocytes function in immunity; leukocytes transport oxygen and carbon dioxide; platelets have no known function at all"],"answer":2,"exp":"Erythrocytes (red blood cells) transport oxygen and carbon dioxide, leukocytes (white blood cells) function in the immune system, and platelets are involved in blood clotting. — Anatomy - Vascular & Lymphatics - Shah Summer I UPDATEDsv-1.pptx, Slide 74"},{"q":"What are the three main plasma protein types, and what percentage of plasma proteins does albumin make up?","choices":["Albumins (60%), globulins (35%), and fibrinogen (4%)","Only two plasma protein types: albumins and globulins","Hemoglobin (60%), albumins (35%), and globulins (4%)","Albumins (4%), globulins (60%), and fibrinogen (35%)"],"answer":0,"exp":"The three main plasma protein types are albumins (60%, major contributors to osmotic pressure, transport lipids/steroid hormones), globulins (35%, transport ions/hormones/lipids, immune function), and fibrinogen (4%, essential for clotting, convertible to insoluble fibrin), plus regulatory proteins (<1%). — Anatomy - Vascular & Lymphatics - Shah Summer I UPDATEDsv-1.pptx, Slide 75"},{"q":"What percentage of body weight does whole blood approximately make up, and what is the normal blood pH range?","choices":["About 7 percent of body weight; pH 7.35-7.45","About 25 percent of body weight; pH 6.0-6.5","About 50 percent of body weight; pH 7.0 exactly","About 1 percent of body weight; pH 8.0-8.5"],"answer":0,"exp":"Whole blood makes up approximately 7 percent of body weight, with a normal pH range of 7.35-7.45. — Anatomy - Vascular & Lymphatics - Shah Summer I UPDATEDsv-1.pptx, Slide 77"},{"q":"What is the difference between hypovolemic, normovolemic, and hypervolemic?","choices":["Hypovolemic: excessive blood volume; normovolemic: low blood volume; hypervolemic: normal blood volume","These terms specifically describe blood pH levels, not blood volume states in the body","Hypovolemic: low blood volume; normovolemic: normal blood volume; hypervolemic: excessive blood volume","All three terms describe the identical blood volume state, each simply meaning normal volume"],"answer":2,"exp":"Hypovolemic describes low blood volume, normovolemic describes normal blood volume, and hypervolemic describes excessive blood volume. — Anatomy - Vascular & Lymphatics - Shah Summer I UPDATEDsv-1.pptx, Slide 77"},{"q":"What percentage of whole blood volume does plasma make up, and what are its three components?","choices":["About 45 percent; consists mostly of formed elements and cellular debris","About 55 percent; consists of 92% water, 7% proteins, and 1% other solutes","About 92 percent; consists almost entirely of dissolved protein alone","About 7 percent; consists almost entirely of pure water alone"],"answer":1,"exp":"Plasma makes up about 55 percent of the volume of whole blood, consisting of 92 percent water, 7 percent proteins (albumin, globulins, fibrinogen, regulatory proteins), and 1 percent other solutes (electrolytes, organic nutrients, organic waste). — Anatomy - Vascular & Lymphatics - Shah Summer I UPDATEDsv-1.pptx, Slide 78"},{"q":"What is the hematocrit (packed cell volume), and what are the normal male versus female values?","choices":["The total blood volume in liters, not a percentage; males average ~5.5 L, females ~4.5 L","The percentage of whole blood occupied by formed elements; males ~45% (40-54%), females ~42% (37-47%)","The percentage of plasma in whole blood, identical for males and females at approximately 55%","A measure of blood pH, not cell volume; normal range is 7.35-7.45 for both sexes"],"answer":1,"exp":"Hematocrit (also called packed cell volume or volume of packed red cells) is the percentage of whole blood occupied by the formed elements; normal values are approximately 45% (40-54%) for males and 42% (37-47%) for females. — Anatomy - Vascular & Lymphatics - Shah Summer I UPDATEDsv-1.pptx, Slide 79"},{"q":"What is the structure of a red blood cell (RBC), and what does it contain?","choices":["A cell shaped like a sphere with a thick central region, retaining a nucleus and mitochondria for its own independent energy production","A cell containing melanin instead of hemoglobin, produced and stored within melanocytes located deep within the bone marrow stroma","A cell with a large, multilobed nucleus and abundant cytoplasmic organelles, structurally similar to a circulating neutrophil","Biconcave disc shape, lacking organelles and a nucleus (anucleated), containing hemoglobin (4 polypeptide subunits: 2 alpha, 2 beta chains, each with heme containing iron)"],"answer":3,"exp":"Red blood cells are biconcave discs with a thin central region, lacking cell organelles and a nucleus (anucleated); they contain hemoglobin, consisting of 4 polypeptide subunits (2 alpha chains and 2 beta chains), each chain containing heme, which contains iron. — Anatomy - Vascular & Lymphatics - Shah Summer I UPDATEDsv-1.pptx, Slide 79"},{"q":"What are the two major classes of leukocytes, and which cell types belong to each?","choices":["Only one class exists: granulocytes","Granulocytes (neutrophils, eosinophils, basophils) and agranulocytes (monocytes, lymphocytes)","Granulocytes (monocytes, lymphocytes) and agranulocytes (neutrophils, eosinophils, basophils)","Erythrocytes and platelets, which are the two major leukocyte classes"],"answer":1,"exp":"The two major classes of leukocytes are granulocytes (granular leukocytes: neutrophils, eosinophils, basophils) and agranulocytes (agranular leukocytes: monocytes, lymphocytes), five major types total. — Anatomy - Vascular & Lymphatics - Shah Summer I UPDATEDsv-1.pptx, Slide 80"},{"q":"What do neutrophil granules contain, and how long do neutrophils typically live?","choices":["No granules at all; live indefinitely","Histamine and heparin; live several years","Chemicals to reduce inflammation; live about 10 days","Chemicals to kill bacteria; live about 10 hours"],"answer":3,"exp":"Neutrophil granules contain chemicals to kill bacteria; neutrophils are typically the first WBC at a bacterial site, are very active phagocytic cells, and only live about 10 hours. — Anatomy - Vascular & Lymphatics - Shah Summer I UPDATEDsv-1.pptx, Slide 81"},{"q":"What do eosinophil granules release, and what do eosinophils typically attack?","choices":["Release chemicals that reduce inflammation; attack foreign substances that have reacted with circulating antibodies (e.g., allergic reactions, parasites)","Release no granule chemicals at all; function only in blood clotting alongside platelets during vascular injury and wound repair","Release histamine and heparin granules; attack bacteria directly through active phagocytosis and enzymatic digestion","Release chemicals to attract fibroblasts to wound sites for repair; attack cancer cells via direct cytotoxic granule release mechanisms"],"answer":0,"exp":"Eosinophil granules release chemicals that reduce inflammation; eosinophils attack foreign substances that have reacted with circulating antibodies, such as in allergic reactions or parasitic infections, and typically have a bilobed nucleus. — Anatomy - Vascular & Lymphatics - Shah Summer I UPDATEDsv-1.pptx, Slide 81"},{"q":"What do basophil granules release, and what do histamine and heparin each do?","choices":["Release chemicals that constrict blood vessels and actively promote blood clotting","Release antibodies, functioning identically to plasma cells in the immune response","Release histamine (dilates blood vessels) and heparin (prevents abnormal blood clotting)","Release chemicals that kill bacteria only, with no other described granule functions"],"answer":2,"exp":"Basophil granules release histamine, which dilates blood vessels, and heparin, which prevents abnormal blood clotting; the nucleus is usually hidden due to all the granules. — Anatomy - Vascular & Lymphatics - Shah Summer I UPDATEDsv-1.pptx, Slide 82"},{"q":"What is distinctive about monocytes, in terms of size, nucleus shape, and function?","choices":["Cells identical in size and function to platelets, releasing clotting factors into the surrounding bloodstream","Small, non-phagocytic cells with a multilobed nucleus, releasing histamine and heparin from granules","Large phagocytic cells with a kidney-shaped or large oval-shaped nucleus, releasing chemicals to attract other phagocytic cells and fibroblasts","Cells that only produce antibodies, never phagocytosing bacteria or cellular debris, and lacking any kidney-shaped nucleus"],"answer":2,"exp":"Monocytes are large phagocytic cells with a kidney-shaped or large oval-shaped nucleus; they release chemicals to attract other phagocytic cells and to attract fibroblasts. — Anatomy - Vascular & Lymphatics - Shah Summer I UPDATEDsv-1.pptx, Slide 82"},{"q":"What are lymphocytes responsible for, and what three cell types can they differentiate into?","choices":["Primary cells of the lymphatic system, responsible for specific immunity; differentiate into T cells, B cells, and NK cells","Cells found only in bone marrow tissue, differentiating into osteocytes and osteoblasts over time","Primary phagocytic cells of the blood; they differentiate directly into neutrophils and eosinophils over time","Cells involved only in blood clotting, differentiating directly into platelets within the bloodstream"],"answer":0,"exp":"Lymphocytes are the primary cells of the lymphatic system, responsible for specific immunity; they can differentiate to form T cells (attack foreign cells directly), B cells (secrete antibodies), and NK cells (immune surveillance). — Anatomy - Vascular & Lymphatics - Shah Summer I UPDATEDsv-1.pptx, Slide 83"},{"q":"What are platelets derived from, and what is thrombocytopenia versus thrombocytosis?","choices":["Derived from megakaryocytes; thrombocytopenia is lower-than-normal platelet count, thrombocytosis is higher-than-normal platelet count","Platelets are not derived from any other cell type at all; thrombocytopenia and thrombocytosis both describe entirely normal counts","Thrombocytopenia and thrombocytosis both describe an identical, entirely normal platelet count in healthy adults","Derived directly from lymphocytes; thrombocytopenia is a higher-than-normal platelet count, thrombocytosis is lower"],"answer":0,"exp":"Platelets (formerly called thrombocytes) are derived from megakaryocytes, which fragment to form membrane-enclosed packets of chemicals (about 350,000 platelets per microliter of blood); thrombocytopenia is a lower-than-normal platelet count, while thrombocytosis is a higher-than-normal platelet count. — Anatomy - Vascular & Lymphatics - Shah Summer I UPDATEDsv-1.pptx, Slide 84"},{"q":"What three things does the lymphatic system consist of?","choices":["Only lymph and circulating blood plasma together","Lymph, lymphatic vessels, and lymphatic tissues and organs","Only lymphatic vessels, with no associated lymphoid organs at all","Erythrocytes, leukocytes, and platelets exclusively"],"answer":1,"exp":"The lymphatic system consists of lymph, lymphatic vessels, and lymphatic tissues and organs. — Anatomy - Vascular & Lymphatics - Shah Summer I UPDATEDsv-1.pptx, Slide 86"},{"q":"What does lymph consist of?","choices":["Interstitial fluid, lymphocytes, and macrophages","Only dissolved plasma proteins present","Only platelets and fibrinogen present","Only red blood cells and clotting factors"],"answer":0,"exp":"Lymph consists of interstitial fluid, lymphocytes, and macrophages. — Anatomy - Vascular & Lymphatics - Shah Summer I UPDATEDsv-1.pptx, Slide 87"},{"q":"What are the primary lymphatic structures, and what are the secondary lymphatic structures?","choices":["Primary: tonsils and lymph nodes; Secondary: thymus, red bone marrow, and spleen","Both categories include identical structures, namely the thymus and red bone marrow","Primary: thymus and red bone marrow; Secondary: tonsils, lymph nodes, spleen, and MALT","Only primary lymphatic structures exist; there are no secondary structures described"],"answer":2,"exp":"Primary lymphatic structures are the thymus and red bone marrow; secondary lymphatic structures are the tonsils, lymph nodes (axillary, lumbar, pelvic, inguinal), spleen, and mucosa-associated lymphoid tissue (MALT). — Anatomy - Vascular & Lymphatics - Shah Summer I UPDATEDsv-1.pptx, Slide 89"},{"q":"What are the listed functions of the lymphatic system?","choices":["Only regulate body temperature through cutaneous sweat gland activity, blood vessel dilation, and shivering thermogenesis mechanisms","Only produce red blood cells within red bone marrow via the ongoing processes of erythropoiesis, iron recycling, and globin synthesis","Only produce digestive enzymes that break down proteins, carbohydrates, and fats within the small intestine's lumen and mucosa","Produce/maintain/distribute lymphocytes, maintain blood volume and interstitial fluid composition, and provide an alternative transport route for hormones/nutrients/waste"],"answer":3,"exp":"The lymphatic system's functions include producing, maintaining, and distributing lymphocytes, maintaining normal blood volume and interstitial fluid composition, and providing an alternative route for the transport of hormones, nutrients, and waste. — Anatomy - Vascular & Lymphatics - Shah Summer I UPDATEDsv-1.pptx, Slide 91"},{"q":"How do lymphatic capillaries compare to vascular (blood) capillaries in diameter and wall thickness?","choices":["Lymphatic capillaries are larger in diameter with thinner walls than vascular capillaries","Lymphatic capillaries are smaller in diameter with thicker walls than vascular capillaries","Both types of capillaries are identical in diameter and wall thickness measurements","Lymphatic capillaries have no walls at all, unlike true vascular capillaries"],"answer":0,"exp":"Lymphatic capillaries are larger in diameter and have thinner walls than vascular capillaries; they also have an irregular outline, anchoring filaments connecting to surrounding connective tissue, and greater permeability. — Anatomy - Vascular & Lymphatics - Shah Summer I UPDATEDsv-1.pptx, Slide 93"},{"q":"How do larger lymphatic vessels (ducts) compare to veins in terms of walls, lumen, and pressure?","choices":["Lymphatic vessels lack valves entirely, unlike veins, and have much thicker walls and higher internal pressure","Lymphatic vessels have thinner walls, larger lumens, and lower pressure than veins, but have valves like most veins","Both vessel types are structurally identical, sharing the same wall thickness, lumen size, and pressure","Lymphatic vessels have thicker walls and higher pressure than veins, and they lack any valves at all"],"answer":1,"exp":"Larger lymphatic vessels have thinner walls, larger lumens than veins, and don't have easily identifiable tunics; pressure in lymphatic vessels is lower than in veins, but larger lymphatic vessels do have valves just like most veins (preventing lymph backflow). — Anatomy - Vascular & Lymphatics - Shah Summer I UPDATEDsv-1.pptx, Slide 94"},{"q":"What two mechanisms help propel lymph toward the venous system?","choices":["Lymph is not actively propelled; it remains stationary until osmotic pressure builds","Only gravity pulling downward, with no muscular or respiratory assistance","Only the heart's direct pumping action upon the lymphatic vessels","Skeletal muscle contraction and decreased thoracic pressure during inhalation"],"answer":3,"exp":"Skeletal muscles contract to help propel lymph, and inhalation decreases thoracic pressure, which helps move lymph toward the venous system (subclavian veins). — Anatomy - Vascular & Lymphatics - Shah Summer I UPDATEDsv-1.pptx, Slide 94"},{"q":"What is the difference between superficial and deep lymphatics?","choices":["Superficial lymphatics collect from visceral organs only; deep lymphatics are found solely within the subcutaneous layer of the skin","Superficial lymphatics are found in the subcutaneous layer and mucous/serous linings; deep lymphatics collect from skeletal muscles and tissues of the neck/limbs/trunk/viscera","Both sets collect from identical locations, namely the skeletal muscles and deep visceral organs alike, with no subcutaneous drainage","Only one single set of lymphatic vessels exists, collecting uniformly from all body tissues without any superficial-deep distinction"],"answer":1,"exp":"Superficial lymphatics are found in the subcutaneous layer, and in the mucous lining of the digestive/respiratory/urinary/reproductive tracts and serous lining of the pleural/pericardial/peritoneal cavities; deep lymphatics collect lymph from skeletal muscles and tissues of the neck, limbs, trunk, and visceral organs. — Anatomy - Vascular & Lymphatics - Shah Summer I UPDATEDsv-1.pptx, Slide 96"},{"q":"What are the five major lymphatic trunks?","choices":["Lumbar, intestinal, bronchomediastinal, subclavian, and jugular trunks","Only two trunks exist: the thoracic duct and right lymphatic duct","Cervical, axillary, and inguinal lymphatic trunks, three in total","Renal, hepatic, and splenic lymphatic trunks, three in total"],"answer":0,"exp":"The five major lymphatic trunks are the lumbar trunks, intestinal trunks, bronchomediastinal trunks, subclavian trunks, and jugular trunks. — Anatomy - Vascular & Lymphatics - Shah Summer I UPDATEDsv-1.pptx, Slide 97"},{"q":"What does the thoracic duct drain, and into what vein does it drain?","choices":["Drains lymph from both sides of the body inferior to the diaphragm and the left side superior to the diaphragm; drains into the left subclavian vein","Drains only the upper body on both sides above the diaphragm; drains into the internal jugular vein directly","Drains the entire body equally from both sides above and below the diaphragm; it drains into the aorta","Drains only the right side of the body above and below the diaphragm; drains into the right subclavian vein"],"answer":0,"exp":"The thoracic duct, the larger lymph duct, drains lymph from both sides of the body inferior to the diaphragm (abdomen, both legs) and the left side of the body superior to the diaphragm (left arm, torso, neck, head); it drains into the left subclavian vein. — Anatomy - Vascular & Lymphatics - Shah Summer I UPDATEDsv-1.pptx, Slide 99"},{"q":"What saclike structure does the thoracic duct begin as, and what does it collect lymph from?","choices":["The cisterna chyli; collects only from the right jugular trunk and right subclavian trunk, on the opposite side","A structure called the lymphatic bulb, collecting lymph only from the spleen and the liver directly via a short connecting duct","The cisterna chyli; collects from left/right lumbar trunks, intestinal trunks, left bronchomediastinal trunk, left subclavian trunk, and left jugular trunk","No saclike structure is involved; the duct begins as a simple, uniform tube along its entire length from the pelvis to the neck"],"answer":2,"exp":"The thoracic duct begins as a saclike structure called the cisterna chyli, which collects lymph from the left and right lumbar trunks, intestinal trunks, the left bronchomediastinal trunk, the left subclavian trunk, and the left jugular trunk. — Anatomy - Vascular & Lymphatics - Shah Summer I UPDATEDsv-1.pptx, Slide 100"},{"q":"What does the right lymphatic duct drain, and into what vein does it drain?","choices":["Drains the entire body via all five major lymphatic trunks; it drains directly into the left subclavian vein near the base of the neck","Drains the lower extremities and pelvis via the lumbar trunks; it drains into the inferior vena cava directly near the kidneys and liver","Drains the right arm, right side of the torso/neck/head (via right bronchomediastinal, right subclavian, and right jugular trunks); drains into the right subclavian vein","Drains only the left arm via the left subclavian trunk; it drains into the right subclavian vein instead of the expected left vein"],"answer":2,"exp":"The right lymphatic duct drains lymph from the right arm, right side of the torso, right side of the neck, and right side of the head (specifically via the right bronchomediastinal trunk, right subclavian trunk, and right jugular trunk); it drains into the right subclavian vein. — Anatomy - Vascular & Lymphatics - Shah Summer I UPDATEDsv-1.pptx, Slide 101"},{"q":"What do T cells (thymus-dependent cells) stand for, and where do they originate versus become activated?","choices":["Originate in the bone marrow but travel to the thymus gland to become activated (immunocompetent) by thymosin","T cells do not require any activation process, remaining fully functional from the moment of production","Originate in the spleen, becoming activated in the bone marrow through thymosin exposure","Originate and become fully activated entirely within the thymus gland, with no bone marrow origin at all"],"answer":0,"exp":"T cells (thymus-dependent cells) originate in the bone marrow but travel to the thymus gland, where they become activated (immunocompetent) by thymosin; mature T cells then leave the thymus and migrate to red bone marrow, spleen, and other lymphatic tissues. — Anatomy - Vascular & Lymphatics - Shah Summer I UPDATEDsv-1.pptx, Slide 103"},{"q":"What are the four different types of T cells described, and their functions?","choices":["Cytotoxic (attack foreign cells/viruses), helper (stimulate T and B cells), regulatory (moderate immune response), and memory (activate upon re-exposure to an antigen)","All four T cell types perform an identical function, each directly attacking foreign cells and invading viruses without specialization","Only cytotoxic and helper T cells exist, with no regulatory or memory subtypes ever described at all within this course material","B cells, plasma cells, cytotoxic cells, and NK cells, listed together as the four distinct T cell types in this material"],"answer":0,"exp":"The four types of T cells are cytotoxic T cells (attack foreign cells and viruses), helper T cells (stimulate both T and B cells, enhance antibody production), regulatory T cells (moderate immune response), and memory T cells (become activated if the same antigen appears later). — Anatomy - Vascular & Lymphatics - Shah Summer I UPDATEDsv-1.pptx, Slide 103"},{"q":"Where do B cells originate and become immunocompetent, and what can they differentiate into?","choices":["Differentiate only into cytotoxic T cells, originating and maturing entirely within the thymus gland","Originate in the thymus, becoming immunocompetent there before differentiating into NK cells alone","B cells cannot differentiate into any other cell type, remaining fixed permanently after bone marrow release","Originate and become immunocompetent in the bone marrow; differentiate into plasma cells (produce antibodies) and memory B cells"],"answer":3,"exp":"B cells originate and become immunocompetent in the bone marrow; mature B cells enter the bloodstream and migrate to peripheral tissues, where they can differentiate into plasma cells (producing antibodies/immunoglobulins that react with antigens) and memory B cells (activated upon re-exposure to the same antigen). — Anatomy - Vascular & Lymphatics - Shah Summer I UPDATEDsv-1.pptx, Slide 104"}]};
